# filipino-family.kb - Filipino / Family
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
pamilya | definition | Pamilya means family, and in Filipino it refers to a household rather than to a set of blood relatives, which is the detail that trips up English speakers. The word covers the people living together, so a grandmother in the same house is pamilya while an aunt living in another province is not, even though she is family in English. The members have specific terms and the choice of term carries respect: ama and ina for parents, and for a male elder ito for an older brother or a male cousin and ita for the older sister or a female cousin, while the words for a younger one differ depending on whether the speaker is male or female. Saying ito to a woman of your parents' generation is a serious error, and the word also doubles as a respectful address to an elder of either gender. The wider kin group is sometimes distinguished with a different word, and in the Philippines the family is often the unit that handles care, finance and decision making together. | household, kin, elder, ito, ita, parents, respect | anong meaning ng pamilya, pamilya meaning, ano ang ito | pamilya | pamilya
kapatid | definition | Kapatid means sibling, and it is the word that covers a brother, a sister, a half-sibling or a cousin, so it is broader than the English word it usually translates. The distinction is made by the term of address rather than by a separate noun, and those terms differ by sex and by relative age: a male addresses an older male as kapatid or manong and an older female as ate, while a female uses manang for an older female and manghino for an older male. The older-woman term also doubles as a respectful address to a female elder who is not related, which is why it appears in offices and shops, and the same is true of the male equivalents. A word sometimes used for a sibling of the same sex and age is a kapatid, and the relationship word is independent of who is talking: two siblings may call each other differently depending on which is older. | sibling, brother, sister, cousin, ate, manang, address | anong meaning ng kapatid, kapatid meaning, ano ang ate | kapatid | kapatid
